WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:05.627
Sherrell Dorsey: Melonie, I'm so honored

00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:07.754
to be sitting and sharing
the stage with you.

00:00:07.754 --> 00:00:11.007
Melonie D. Parker: I couldn't wait
for today to have our conversation

00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:12.759
joined with all these lovely women.

00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:15.011
SD: There's so much to cover.

00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:18.848
To be at this experience,

00:00:18.974 --> 00:00:23.186
to be celebrating the power,
the voice, the ideas of women.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.399
There's also some really grappling

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.485
and gripping conversations
that we have to have,

00:00:30.485 --> 00:00:32.571
particularly around diversity,

00:00:32.612 --> 00:00:35.740
this idea of equity, as well as inclusion.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.413
And your work, even beyond your title,
has been extremely massive.

00:00:42.622 --> 00:00:47.627
But we also have to look
at what the last few years has entailed

00:00:47.669 --> 00:00:51.006
and what commitments have been
made from corporate entities.

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:54.384
And so I want to dive in today,
with you this morning,

00:00:54.426 --> 00:00:58.346
about where we sort of were

00:00:58.388 --> 00:01:00.515
and where we're going.

00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:04.769
But maybe let's start
with this commitment,

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.106
particularly that Google made
right out of the gate,

00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:10.775
particularly when we think about 2020,

00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:12.444
the murder of George Floyd

00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:16.656
and the sort of subsequent
reckonings that our country,

00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:18.992
that the world has had

00:01:19.034 --> 00:01:21.745
and what that's meant for your team,

00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:23.788
for your organization at large,

00:01:23.788 --> 00:01:26.833
and how we're starting to think
a little bit differently

00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:28.793
about the shifts that are now happening.

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:30.962
MDP: So it's a great question.

00:01:31.004 --> 00:01:33.840
And even before we get to like,

00:01:33.882 --> 00:01:36.801
the horrific murder of George Floyd,

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:39.137
I think we need to step back
a little bit further

00:01:39.137 --> 00:01:40.847
and think about COVID

00:01:40.889 --> 00:01:44.684
and the comorbidities
that were exacerbated.

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:48.772
You know, we thought
about who was killed by COVID.

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:53.610
And I think that brought
a spotlight that exacerbated

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.239
when we started to look
at police brutality

00:01:58.281 --> 00:02:00.200
and, you know, before George Floyd,

00:02:00.241 --> 00:02:03.495
there was Breonna Taylor, Ahmaud Arbery.

00:02:03.536 --> 00:02:06.873
And this wasn't like an episodic event.

00:02:06.915 --> 00:02:12.379
It really led to a global
racial equity movement.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.214
And I think global here is important.

00:02:14.214 --> 00:02:17.592
So I have four generations
in my household.

00:02:17.634 --> 00:02:19.135
My mom's 85,

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:23.890
the youngest in my household,
I'm a new Gigi to a granddaughter,

00:02:23.932 --> 00:02:26.726
and my mom said --

00:02:26.768 --> 00:02:30.021
who marched in the 1960s
with Dr Martin Luther King,

00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:31.690
both of my parents did, she said,

00:02:31.731 --> 00:02:37.487
"I can't believe that people
outside of the United States

00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:42.409
care about police brutality
towards Black Americans."

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.577
I mean, she was stunned by this.

00:02:44.619 --> 00:02:47.122
And so I think that backdrop is important.

00:02:47.122 --> 00:02:51.418
So what we did at Google,
like, quickly recognizing,

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:55.380
hey, this isn't a series
of episodic events, this is a movement.

00:02:55.422 --> 00:02:58.842
And we need to immediately deepen
the work that we're doing.

00:02:58.883 --> 00:03:05.306
So we went on a 100-day sprint
as a company, not just my team,

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:07.559
which is a very representative team,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.271
I have a rock-star team
that enables everything that I do.

00:03:11.312 --> 00:03:12.856
But this was a company.

00:03:12.897 --> 00:03:17.360
So we did a call to action
globally, across Google,

00:03:17.402 --> 00:03:24.284
and our goal was to really ensure
that we were building sustainable equity

00:03:24.325 --> 00:03:26.536
internally and externally.

00:03:26.578 --> 00:03:30.123
And so we had teams that looked
at all of our products

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:33.168
and how do we use our products
for social good,

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:35.378
which is embedded
in the mission of Google,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.381
to ensure that our products
are helpful and accessible.

00:03:38.423 --> 00:03:40.759
And then internally looking at --

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.137
we used our Black leadership,
our Black executives

00:03:44.137 --> 00:03:45.638
and our Black Googler Network,

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:48.016
which is our employee resource group,

00:03:48.057 --> 00:03:52.937
to look at what do we need to do
internally to build equity

00:03:52.937 --> 00:03:57.567
and not just across the Black community,
but across underrepresented minority.

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:02.030
So we look at Black, Latinx,
Hispanic population

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.324
as well as Indigenous community.

00:04:04.365 --> 00:04:09.954
Out of that, we had eight commitments:
four external, four internal.

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:14.751
We've invested over 500 million dollars
in the racial equity commitments.

00:04:14.751 --> 00:04:17.462
And they complete in 2025.

00:04:17.504 --> 00:04:21.549
So we've learned a lot along the way.

00:04:21.591 --> 00:04:26.513
And we do this work as a collective
with civil rights organizations,

00:04:26.513 --> 00:04:28.723
human rights organizations, you know,

00:04:28.765 --> 00:04:30.391
other organizations,

00:04:30.391 --> 00:04:34.229
because you can't do
this work in isolation.

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:37.023
This work, we have to go together.

00:04:37.482 --> 00:04:40.610
SD: What's been really -- first of all,
that's really fascinating.

00:04:40.652 --> 00:04:42.987
And thank you for really
spelling that out for us.

00:04:43.404 --> 00:04:46.783
What's really interesting is,

00:04:46.783 --> 00:04:53.206
there is almost this new identity around
what discrimination, what marginalization

00:04:53.206 --> 00:04:57.460
and even racism, if we can just call it
by the the term that it is,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.547
has inflicted on various groups.

00:05:00.547 --> 00:05:04.801
And it is sometimes a challenge to think

00:05:04.843 --> 00:05:08.972
that it took a series
of very catastrophic events,

00:05:09.013 --> 00:05:13.309
including a tremendous
public health crisis,

00:05:13.309 --> 00:05:17.438
to get to a place where
our ears were open, right?

00:05:17.438 --> 00:05:22.527
And even when I think about the nature
of the partnerships that you all have had,

00:05:22.527 --> 00:05:25.238
particularly when it comes to HBCUs,

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.240
which you are an alum.

00:05:27.282 --> 00:05:29.075
MDP: Proud Hampton University alum.

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:30.660
SD: Any HBCUs?

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.246
I heard there are some HBCU students.

00:05:33.288 --> 00:05:36.708
MDP: I love all HBCUs,
but particular to ...

00:05:36.749 --> 00:05:39.836
SD: So I'm not part of the club
because I did not attend an HBCU.

00:05:39.836 --> 00:05:42.547
But I hope one day to be an honorary
member of Spelman.

00:05:42.547 --> 00:05:44.257
I should have been a Spelman woman.

00:05:44.257 --> 00:05:45.300
(Cheers)

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.426
Alright, I see, OK.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:48.803
MDP: I'm sure Dr Gayle
is beaming right now.

00:05:48.845 --> 00:05:50.221
Love to have you at Spelman.

00:05:50.221 --> 00:05:51.639
SD: I just need a sweatshirt.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.684
So meet me after, meet me after.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.855
But there are some unique
partnerships that are expanding

00:05:58.897 --> 00:06:01.441
beyond just social justice initiatives

00:06:01.482 --> 00:06:05.278
and even stepping
into an area of education.

00:06:05.778 --> 00:06:09.115
And very unique partnerships,
particularly around the future of work,

00:06:09.157 --> 00:06:11.743
which, when we think about inequalities,

00:06:11.743 --> 00:06:13.703
when we talk about the racial wealth gap,

00:06:13.745 --> 00:06:16.247
when we talk about who gets
to build the future,

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:18.166
who gets to imagine a future,

00:06:18.166 --> 00:06:21.294
a lot of times when we think
about the folks who are left out,

00:06:21.294 --> 00:06:24.589
that is women, that is other
marginalized folks of color

00:06:24.631 --> 00:06:27.258
who don't necessarily get access
in the same way

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.135
that some of our counterparts do.

00:06:29.177 --> 00:06:33.389
And there's a particular emphasis
on unique partnerships,

00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:37.352
in order to create, upskill, reskill,

00:06:37.393 --> 00:06:41.022
or even teach particular skill sets
that are going to be found in the future.

00:06:41.022 --> 00:06:42.982
Can you speak to that a little bit more?

00:06:43.024 --> 00:06:47.779
MDP: Yes, and so it's an incredible
observation and insight.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.657
You know, let's start
with our historically Black colleges

00:06:50.698 --> 00:06:53.534
and universities,
which are national treasures.

00:06:53.576 --> 00:06:58.247
And we have to surround our HBCUs

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:02.043
and ensure that we continue the pipeline.

00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:07.090
And part of that, yes,
is investing in the pipeline

00:07:07.131 --> 00:07:09.592
for development to make sure
that we're hiring.

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:12.303
But there's a bigger part to this picture.

00:07:12.345 --> 00:07:17.141
And so at Google, I lead
an HBCU President's Council.

00:07:17.183 --> 00:07:19.018
And one of the things we're doing,

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:23.523
in addition to creating opportunities
for internships and hiring,

00:07:23.523 --> 00:07:27.443
is we are helping to amplify
research opportunities.

00:07:27.485 --> 00:07:30.822
And so, I'm not sure
if you know this, Sherrell,

00:07:30.822 --> 00:07:34.409
but out of the HBCU landscape,

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:39.163
there are no HBCUs currently
that are at R1 research standing,

00:07:39.163 --> 00:07:42.583
and that means that we have
a stewardship responsibility

00:07:42.625 --> 00:07:45.378
to ensure that we're providing
research opportunities,

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.464
but research opportunities also not --

00:07:48.506 --> 00:07:50.675
They don't just provide development,

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:54.554
they also provide diverse voices,

00:07:54.554 --> 00:07:59.851
diverse ideas, diverse thoughts
into cutting-edge research.

00:07:59.892 --> 00:08:03.604
But we just launched
an applied research institute

00:08:03.646 --> 00:08:09.027
a couple of weeks ago,
in partnership with UNCF and TMCF,

00:08:09.027 --> 00:08:14.323
focused on HBCUs with mathematical
and computational research

00:08:14.365 --> 00:08:16.826
to provide these hands-on opportunities.

00:08:16.868 --> 00:08:19.912
And with Spelman College
through Google.org,

00:08:19.954 --> 00:08:26.002
we provided a five million-dollar grant
for a women of color STEAM database.

00:08:26.753 --> 00:08:28.463
No one else is doing this.

00:08:28.504 --> 00:08:31.174
And so Google developers,

00:08:31.215 --> 00:08:35.011
other HBCUs are partnering
hand in hand with Spelman

00:08:35.011 --> 00:08:36.929
to create this database.

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:42.393
And this is the type of opportunity
that we have to continue to provide.

00:08:42.435 --> 00:08:44.854
And just, you know, one other example,

00:08:44.854 --> 00:08:46.647
at Howard University,

00:08:46.689 --> 00:08:49.984
we have a partnership
that we just announced.

00:08:49.984 --> 00:08:52.612
It's called Project Elevate Black Voices.

00:08:52.653 --> 00:08:57.700
So how many of you are familiar
with automatic speech technology?

00:08:57.742 --> 00:08:59.035
And I don't know about you,

00:08:59.077 --> 00:09:01.704
sometimes I use it
and it doesn't recognize my voice.

00:09:01.746 --> 00:09:05.083
And so Google's research,
as well as external research,

00:09:05.083 --> 00:09:07.960
you know, has determined and validated

00:09:08.002 --> 00:09:11.672
that it doesn't universally
recognize all voices.

00:09:11.714 --> 00:09:15.802
So Howard University
holds the licensing rights,

00:09:15.802 --> 00:09:19.639
but they are doing that research
to improve the technology.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.476
These are the types of opportunities
and unique partnerships

00:09:23.518 --> 00:09:27.021
that we have to have
to not only build the pipeline,

00:09:27.063 --> 00:09:30.566
but to ensure that we have
diversity in the design,

00:09:30.608 --> 00:09:32.735
in the products and in the services.

00:09:33.277 --> 00:09:35.154
SD: That's super incredible,

00:09:35.196 --> 00:09:39.951
particularly for students
who are currently at these institutions

00:09:39.992 --> 00:09:42.078
and are going to get access
and opportunity.

00:09:42.078 --> 00:09:45.331
I think also to Google executives
and employees as well,

00:09:45.331 --> 00:09:48.000
who provide mentorship
and leadership as well

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.128
amongst these particular programs.

00:09:50.169 --> 00:09:54.799
And you talked about the partnerships
with the United Negro College Fund,

00:09:54.841 --> 00:09:57.009
the Thurgood Marshall Foundation,

00:09:57.009 --> 00:10:00.054
and oddly enough, in 2020 --

00:10:00.096 --> 00:10:02.056
And I always want to go back to that date

00:10:02.098 --> 00:10:07.186
because there were some
really interesting realities.

00:10:07.728 --> 00:10:09.021
People, for the first time,

00:10:09.063 --> 00:10:12.358
we're learning about all of these
organizations that were supporting

00:10:12.358 --> 00:10:15.486
and have been doing work for decades.

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:20.449
So for a lot of folks,
that was kind of a first-hand discovery.

00:10:20.491 --> 00:10:26.581
So when I think about this area
of DE and I and sort of 2020 to 2021,

00:10:26.622 --> 00:10:31.085
we saw sort of the largest growth
of chief diversity officers being named

00:10:31.127 --> 00:10:33.963
for the first time in corporate spaces.

00:10:33.963 --> 00:10:40.428
We saw this kind of deluge of focus
on things like a 15-percent pledge

00:10:40.428 --> 00:10:45.183
across retail or representation on stages

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:49.312
or even within authorship and books.

00:10:49.645 --> 00:10:51.689
And then we hit 2022.

00:10:52.523 --> 00:10:57.028
And we saw this sort of decline
economically, of course,

00:10:57.028 --> 00:11:00.823
housing to inflation
to all of these things

00:11:00.865 --> 00:11:03.201
that started to take precedence,

00:11:03.242 --> 00:11:07.622
which also meant the pullback
of all of those big promises,

00:11:07.663 --> 00:11:09.332
those hyper focuses.

00:11:09.332 --> 00:11:14.003
And the shift was very palpable
for so many of us.

00:11:14.003 --> 00:11:16.172
And we kind of knew
that it was going to come.

00:11:16.214 --> 00:11:18.591
We knew that the focus and the language

00:11:18.633 --> 00:11:24.263
and almost the hysteria around
how do we elevate marginalized voices

00:11:24.305 --> 00:11:25.681
might take a turn.

00:11:25.723 --> 00:11:27.308
So we sort of had to think about,

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.562
OK, if we're going to have this spotlight
for like, 12 to 18 months,

00:11:31.562 --> 00:11:33.940
like, how do we, like,
rush out and get it done?

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:38.236
What's fascinating is you talk
about these pillars,

00:11:38.277 --> 00:11:40.404
these eight pillars and these goals

00:11:40.446 --> 00:11:43.074
that are going to be accomplished by 2025,

00:11:43.115 --> 00:11:44.742
which is absolutely fascinating.

00:11:45.201 --> 00:11:50.164
And so I want to get an understanding of

00:11:50.206 --> 00:11:55.127
why has this work stayed the course,

00:11:55.169 --> 00:11:58.256
and where are we going.

00:11:58.714 --> 00:12:02.760
Because I hear these amazing
initiatives and opportunities.

00:12:02.802 --> 00:12:04.553
I hear of the dedication.

00:12:04.595 --> 00:12:09.642
You're one of the few remaining
chief diversity officers, unfortunately,

00:12:09.642 --> 00:12:12.061
that's really heading a major company.

00:12:14.313 --> 00:12:19.735
But I think that many of us might be
concerned about, is this a fad,

00:12:19.777 --> 00:12:21.862
are we going to have to
change language now?

00:12:21.862 --> 00:12:24.240
We're only talking about ESG now?

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.867
We're only talking about terms
in a certain kind of way,

00:12:26.909 --> 00:12:28.911
because now all of the language we learned

00:12:28.953 --> 00:12:32.039
in the last two years
is somehow deemed offensive?

00:12:32.623 --> 00:12:37.837
So maybe you can paint a picture
of hope in some cases.

00:12:37.837 --> 00:12:39.297
MDP: I was just thinking that.

00:12:39.338 --> 00:12:41.340
So let me let me interject with some hope.

00:12:41.382 --> 00:12:46.512
So what we're seeing today,
I don't lose heart.

00:12:46.554 --> 00:12:47.680
I do have hope.

00:12:47.722 --> 00:12:49.223
I mean, my parents, I told you,

00:12:49.265 --> 00:12:53.102
marched in the 1960s
with Dr Martin Luther King.

00:12:53.144 --> 00:12:56.022
And so we've seen this in history before.

00:12:56.022 --> 00:12:59.734
One of the things that we've normalized
that we're not going to go back from,

00:12:59.734 --> 00:13:03.112
we talk openly about race
in the workplace.

00:13:03.154 --> 00:13:05.489
I mean, if you think about,

00:13:05.531 --> 00:13:10.703
particularly Black and Brown communities
are either hyper visible or invisible.

00:13:10.745 --> 00:13:13.164
In between that,
it's a whole lot of stress.

00:13:13.164 --> 00:13:14.665
And so what we're talking about

00:13:14.665 --> 00:13:20.254
is how do we normalize Black
and Brown communities in the context?

00:13:20.254 --> 00:13:23.341
And that's what we've done
following the murder of George Floyd.

00:13:23.341 --> 00:13:26.010
I think that's also why
we've seen some of the pushback.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.263
But we're not going to go back
to where we [were].

00:13:30.139 --> 00:13:33.267
I have a lot of hope with Gen X,

00:13:33.309 --> 00:13:35.436
the way that, you know,

00:13:35.436 --> 00:13:40.107
they have forced a demand
for how we interact,

00:13:40.107 --> 00:13:42.902
what they want, how they show up.

00:13:42.902 --> 00:13:45.654
And we're really leaning into that.

00:13:45.696 --> 00:13:51.911
And so I want to make sure
that we don't operate out of fear

00:13:51.952 --> 00:13:55.456
and that we don't retract,
that we stand strong.

00:13:55.498 --> 00:13:58.959
And Google is standing strong
and unapologetic

00:13:58.959 --> 00:14:00.378
about the commitment we make.

00:14:00.378 --> 00:14:01.629
And we're able to do that.

00:14:01.629 --> 00:14:03.714
And I'm able to sit in my role

00:14:03.714 --> 00:14:07.301
because we have an incredible CEO
with Sundar Pichai

00:14:07.343 --> 00:14:10.137
who really gets it, understands it,

00:14:10.137 --> 00:14:11.597
and it's a company imperative.

00:14:11.597 --> 00:14:13.474
So we have racial equity goals,

00:14:13.516 --> 00:14:17.103
but we also have company-wide
goals as well.

00:14:17.103 --> 00:14:21.982
And our CEO meets with our HBCU
Presidents Council as well

00:14:22.024 --> 00:14:24.318
and spends and pours time there.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:29.365
And so these are the types
of things that we have to hold on to.

00:14:29.365 --> 00:14:32.159
But they're also the things
that we have to push for

00:14:32.201 --> 00:14:36.330
and to continue to demand fairness
and respect and dignity,

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:40.918
which go hand in hand with equity
and diversity and inclusion.

00:14:41.919 --> 00:14:45.673
And there's a lot of talk
now about belonging.

00:14:45.673 --> 00:14:48.634
And I think we should hit this head on.

00:14:48.676 --> 00:14:51.220
I believe that diversity,
equity and inclusion

00:14:51.262 --> 00:14:53.139
is insufficient without belonging.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:57.476
Like, insufficient without
recognizing that everybody --

00:14:58.018 --> 00:15:03.441
The science of belonging says
that people have to feel rewarded, seen,

00:15:03.482 --> 00:15:09.029
recognized and valued
in order to feel like they belong.

00:15:09.071 --> 00:15:12.366
And so I think we see this added on.

00:15:12.366 --> 00:15:17.788
And so these are the types
of competencies and capabilities

00:15:17.788 --> 00:15:20.332
that are here to stay,

00:15:20.374 --> 00:15:21.750
even though we get pushback.

00:15:21.792 --> 00:15:24.545
But pushback means
there's progress happening

00:15:24.587 --> 00:15:27.173
and the research shows
that there's progress happening.

00:15:27.173 --> 00:15:29.717
SD: A little discomfort is good
for all of us, right?

00:15:29.717 --> 00:15:31.719
A little discomfort is good for the soul.

00:15:31.719 --> 00:15:33.304
Anybody been uncomfortable? Yeah.

00:15:33.345 --> 00:15:36.348
Alright, major growth spurts
happening around here.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:38.350
I love how you talk about,

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:41.520
or you brought in
that concept of belonging.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.609
And also highlighted the different
requirements of generations.

00:15:46.609 --> 00:15:49.778
I think, you know, I'm considered
a geriatric millennial.

00:15:49.778 --> 00:15:51.780
Is anyone else here
a geriatric millennial?

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:52.781
(Scattered applause)

00:15:52.781 --> 00:15:54.325
Yes! Shout out, yes.

00:15:54.325 --> 00:15:57.036
MDP: I'm Gen X, so where are 
my Gen X people? (Laughs)

00:15:57.036 --> 00:15:59.246
(Scattered applause)

00:15:59.288 --> 00:16:01.624
SD: Kids of the '90s, yes.

00:16:02.333 --> 00:16:06.212
I mean, because we remember
before there was internet, right?

00:16:06.545 --> 00:16:09.715
OK, alright, just making sure
I have family in the room here.

00:16:10.799 --> 00:16:15.888
But I love that our generation
started to ask for more.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.682
More balance, more recognition,

00:16:18.724 --> 00:16:21.143
more sustainability, more consciousness

00:16:21.185 --> 00:16:23.395
around how we show up
in the world, in society,

00:16:23.437 --> 00:16:24.688
as well as on the planet.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.774
And I think you referenced as well, Gen Z.

00:16:26.815 --> 00:16:29.652
Gen Z asking for -- like Gen Z is bold.

00:16:29.652 --> 00:16:31.320
Gen Z, shout out.

00:16:31.362 --> 00:16:33.531
(Cheers)

00:16:34.573 --> 00:16:35.908
I love it, I love it.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.869
Maybe y'all will help me put
some stuff on TikTok later today.

00:16:38.911 --> 00:16:40.663
So just catch me backstage, OK?

00:16:42.122 --> 00:16:45.334
But that belonging component

00:16:45.376 --> 00:16:50.130
is a force multiple with Gen Z.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.550
It is "I'm going to show up
and be all who I am."

00:16:52.591 --> 00:16:55.094
MDP: It's a requirement,
it's not an entitlement.

00:16:55.135 --> 00:16:56.845
When I grew up, you know,

00:16:56.887 --> 00:17:02.977
my parents raised me -- my brother and I
are first-generation college,

00:17:03.018 --> 00:17:05.229
I'm first generation corporate America.

00:17:05.229 --> 00:17:09.733
And we were taught
to be better, faster, smarter,

00:17:09.775 --> 00:17:11.068
but to compete at average.

00:17:11.068 --> 00:17:13.195
That never sat right with me.

00:17:13.737 --> 00:17:18.117
But I was taught to fit in, not add in.

00:17:18.158 --> 00:17:20.995
Gen Z is saying, here's where I add in,

00:17:21.036 --> 00:17:25.165
and this is what we have to do
as corporations and organizations.

00:17:25.165 --> 00:17:27.501
It's not about who fits into the culture,

00:17:27.501 --> 00:17:30.462
it's what's missing in the culture
and who adds in the culture.

00:17:30.462 --> 00:17:31.463
(Applause)

00:17:31.463 --> 00:17:33.048
SD: That's an amazing point.

00:17:33.048 --> 00:17:35.217
And I love that you talked
about the belonging

00:17:35.259 --> 00:17:40.055
because it's less
of the quota of diversity, right?

00:17:40.055 --> 00:17:43.851
It's more than just, I'm a data point
that potentially represents something.

00:17:43.892 --> 00:17:45.269
MDP: Much broader than that.

00:17:45.269 --> 00:17:46.645
It's much broader than that.

00:17:46.687 --> 00:17:49.648
I mean, in talking about culture add,

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:52.943
and we've trained over 10,000
managers at Google

00:17:52.985 --> 00:17:56.905
on how to interview for additive skills,

00:17:56.947 --> 00:18:00.534
not just fitting in,
because that's a mindset shift

00:18:00.576 --> 00:18:04.872
and change management
that we have to do on our side,

00:18:04.913 --> 00:18:06.749
from a corporate America side,

00:18:06.749 --> 00:18:10.169
to ensure that we're taking full advantage

00:18:10.169 --> 00:18:12.588
of this incredible talent
that's in the world.

00:18:12.588 --> 00:18:14.632
SD: And so, just as a closing,

00:18:14.673 --> 00:18:17.134
what is your advice to other leaders

00:18:17.176 --> 00:18:20.387
that are struggling to figure out

00:18:20.429 --> 00:18:24.058
how do we create this new culture?

00:18:24.058 --> 00:18:27.061
How do we still prioritize
what diversity looks like,

00:18:27.102 --> 00:18:28.937
what belonging looks like,

00:18:28.937 --> 00:18:31.940
and continue to even just measure
the progress as we go along?

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:34.276
MDP: My advice is to recognize

00:18:34.318 --> 00:18:38.072
what we're talking about
are universal solutions

00:18:38.113 --> 00:18:40.949
that are common to humanity.

00:18:40.991 --> 00:18:42.993
But we have to recognize

00:18:43.035 --> 00:18:47.206
that there are communities
within the universe

00:18:47.247 --> 00:18:49.249
that are situated differently

00:18:49.249 --> 00:18:53.921
and require different treatment
to take full advantage.

00:18:53.962 --> 00:18:56.924
This is a basic right
and a basic privilege.

00:18:56.924 --> 00:18:59.843
And if we could all, as leaders, stand

00:18:59.885 --> 00:19:02.971
and take stock of that
and lift as we climb

00:19:03.013 --> 00:19:05.349
and ensure we're bringing everybody along,

00:19:05.349 --> 00:19:08.644
we will reach the culture
that we aspire to.

00:19:08.686 --> 00:19:10.771
SD: That's incredible, yes, thank you.

00:19:10.813 --> 00:19:12.064
(Applause)

00:19:12.064 --> 00:19:15.025
Well, we see why
you are such an amazing boss.

00:19:15.025 --> 00:19:16.026
MDP: Thank you.

00:19:16.026 --> 00:19:18.570
SD: We get to witness it live,
this is incredible.

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:19.822
Melonie, thank you so much

00:19:19.863 --> 00:19:23.909
for joining us here on the stage
today at TEDWomen.

00:19:23.951 --> 00:19:26.662
Thank you for everyone here as well.

00:19:26.954 --> 00:19:32.501
And thank you to the HBCU students
who are also in the room representing.

00:19:33.836 --> 00:19:35.254
MDP: Thank you, thank you.

00:19:35.295 --> 00:19:36.463
SD: Thanks everyone.

00:19:36.505 --> 00:19:37.631
(Applause)

